  "narrative": "During the 2010s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture continued to thrive as a celebration of their identity and heritage. The natural hair movement, which originated in the 1960s but resurged in popularity in the 2000s, encouraged individuals to embrace their afro-textured hair, particularly in the workplace. Hairstyles such as Afros, cornrows, box braids, and locs became increasingly popular, allowing women to express themselves authentically and proudly. These styles not only provided a means of self-expression but also served as protective hairstyles, designed to minimize manipulation and exposure of the hair to environmental elements. As a result, Black and African women's hair culture continued to evolve, with an emphasis on embracing natural textures and celebrating the diversity of African diasporic beauty standards.",
